Q: How can I yield list elements infinitely? A: <p>The idiomatic Python way of doing that, as put in some answers, is to use itertools.cycle: that will simply keep yielding results and going through then as you keep fetching the items (which a <code>for</code> loop will do for you):</p>
<pre><code>from itertools import cycle

def gen():
    lst = [&quot;dir_1&quot;, &quot;dir_2&quot;, ..., &quot;dir_n&quot;]
    for i in cycle(lst):
        yield i

</code></pre>
<p>Note that while the use of <code>itertools.cycle</code> will look more idiomatic to Python that  will create an internal cache of the elements yielded in the first iteration and always repeat those - so, if there is any concurrent code updating the list (or, if instead of a list, you plug a filesystem query like <code>os.listdir()</code> you can use a <code>while True</code> block in your generator, and a <code>for</code> loop inside it to repeatedly yield live valus:</p>
<p>Script that yields the living child-directories of a directory, repeteadly:</p>
<pre><code>from itertools import cycle
from pathlib import Path

def gen(root_folder):
    while True:
        for path in Path(root_folder).glob(&quot;*&quot;):
        if not path.is_dir():
            continue
        yield path

</code></pre>

Q: ReactJS AssemblyAI Speaker Labeling Transcription A: <p>The good news: your API call already has <code>speaker_labels: true</code>, so the speaker data is already in the response. The <code>sentiment_analysis_results</code> array includes a <code>speaker</code> field on each result when speaker diarization is enabled — you just need to surface it in your components.</p>
<p>Here's what the response actually looks like:</p>
<pre><code>{
  &quot;sentiment_analysis_results&quot;: [
    {
      &quot;text&quot;: &quot;Smoke from hundreds of wildfires in Canada is triggering air quality alerts.&quot;,
      &quot;start&quot;: 250,
      &quot;end&quot;: 5180,
      &quot;sentiment&quot;: &quot;NEGATIVE&quot;,
      &quot;confidence&quot;: 0.943,
      &quot;speaker&quot;: &quot;A&quot;
    },
    {
      &quot;text&quot;: &quot;The haze makes the sky look like something out of a movie.&quot;,
      &quot;start&quot;: 5400,
      &quot;end&quot;: 9200,
      &quot;sentiment&quot;: &quot;NEUTRAL&quot;,
      &quot;confidence&quot;: 0.871,
      &quot;speaker&quot;: &quot;B&quot;
    }
  ],
  &quot;utterances&quot;: [
    {
      &quot;speaker&quot;: &quot;A&quot;,
      &quot;text&quot;: &quot;Smoke from hundreds of wildfires...&quot;,
      &quot;start&quot;: 250,
      &quot;end&quot;: 5180
    }
  ]
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Update <code>Highlighted.js</code></strong> to accept and display the speaker label:</p>
<pre><code>import { Box, Text, Tooltip } from '@chakra-ui/react';

const sentimentColor = {
  POSITIVE: 'lightgreen',
  NEGATIVE: 'pink.200',
  NEUTRAL: 'lightgray',
};

const Highlighted = ({ text, sentiment, speaker }) =&gt; {
  return (
    &lt;Tooltip label={`Sentiment: ${sentiment}`} hasArrow&gt;
      &lt;Box
        as=&quot;mark&quot;
        bg={sentimentColor[sentiment]}
        mr=&quot;1&quot;
        mb=&quot;2&quot;
        px=&quot;1&quot;
        borderRadius=&quot;sm&quot;
        display=&quot;inline-flex&quot;
        alignItems=&quot;center&quot;
        gap=&quot;1&quot;
      &gt;
        {/* Speaker badge */}
        {speaker &amp;&amp; (
          &lt;Text
            as=&quot;span&quot;
            fontSize=&quot;xs&quot;
            fontWeight=&quot;bold&quot;
            bg=&quot;whiteAlpha.600&quot;
            px=&quot;1&quot;
            borderRadius=&quot;sm&quot;
            flexShrink={0}
          &gt;
            {speaker}
          &lt;/Text&gt;
        )}
        {text}
      &lt;/Box&gt;
    &lt;/Tooltip&gt;
  );
};

export default Highlighted;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Update <code>Result.js</code></strong> to pass <code>speaker</code> through:</p>
<pre><code>import { Box, Text, Divider, Badge } from '@chakra-ui/react';
import Highlighted from './Highlighted';

const Result = ({ transcript }) =&gt; {
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      {/* Sentiment-highlighted transcript with speaker labels */}
      &lt;Box
        width={{ base: '300px', md: '700px', lg: '1000px' }}
        textAlign=&quot;justify&quot;
        mb={6}
      &gt;
        &lt;Text
          fontSize={{ base: '15px', md: '20px', lg: '22px' }}
          lineHeight=&quot;tall&quot;
        &gt;
          {transcript.sentiment_analysis_results.map((result, index) =&gt; (
            &lt;Highlighted
              key={index}
              text={result.text}
              sentiment={result.sentiment}
              speaker={result.speaker}  // ← add this
            /&gt;
          ))}
        &lt;/Text&gt;
      &lt;/Box&gt;

      &lt;Divider my={4} /&gt;

      {/* Clean speaker-turn view using utterances */}
      {transcript.utterances &amp;&amp; (
        &lt;Box
          width={{ base: '300px', md: '700px', lg: '1000px' }}
          textAlign=&quot;left&quot;
        &gt;
          &lt;Text fontWeight=&quot;bold&quot; mb={3} fontSize=&quot;lg&quot;&gt;
            Speaker Breakdown
          &lt;/Text&gt;
          {transcript.utterances.map((utterance, index) =&gt; (
            &lt;Box key={index} mb={3} display=&quot;flex&quot; alignItems=&quot;flex-start&quot; gap={3}&gt;
              &lt;Badge
                colorScheme=&quot;purple&quot;
                fontSize=&quot;sm&quot;
                px={2}
                py={1}
                borderRadius=&quot;md&quot;
                flexShrink={0}
              &gt;
                Speaker {utterance.speaker}
              &lt;/Badge&gt;
              &lt;Text fontSize=&quot;md&quot;&gt;{utterance.text}&lt;/Text&gt;
            &lt;/Box&gt;
          ))}
        &lt;/Box&gt;
      )}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
};

export default Result;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>What changed and why:</strong></p>
<p>ChangeReasonresult.speaker passed to Highlightedsentiment_analysis_results already includes speaker when speaker_labels: trueSpeaker badge inside each highlightVisually ties speaker identity to sentiment-colored texttranscript.utterances section belowCleaner view of full speaker turns without sentence-by-sentence color splittingTooltip on each highlightShows the sentiment label on hover for clarity</p>
<p><strong>No changes needed to <code>App.js</code></strong> — your API call already requests all the right features:</p>
<pre><code>const { data } = await assemblyApi.post('/transcript', {
  audio_url: uploadResponse.upload_url,
  speaker_labels: true,       // ✅ already set — enables result.speaker
  sentiment_analysis: true,   // ✅ already set
  entity_detection: true,
  iab_categories: true,
});
</code></pre>
<p>The only thing to watch: <code>utterances</code> can be <code>null</code> if no speech is detected, so guard against it (the <code>transcript.utterances &amp;&amp;</code> check above handles this).</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p><em>Disclosure: I work at AssemblyAI. This answer uses our batch transcription API — if you need real-time transcription with speaker diarization as audio is recorded, <a href="https://www.assemblyai.com/docs/universal-streaming" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Universal-3 Pro Streaming</a> supports streaming diarization with sub-300ms latency.</em></p>
</blockquote>

Q: Automatically tie a UnityEngine.Profiling profiler sample to every method in a class A: <h3>Marker Scope Disposable (using block)</h3>
<p>As mentioned built-in in there is nothing like that.</p>
<p>Sure you can enable <code>Deep Profile</code> which will basically do exactly what you want - but for each and every type, not only a single one.</p>
<p>The Profiler is kept &quot;manual&quot; intentionally for better control.</p>
<p>So with pure built-in / simple tools the <code>using</code> scopes <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79878128/7111561">as mentioned in your previous question</a> is the fastest, most straight forward you can go if you anyway need this only once.</p>
<hr />
<h3>IL Weaving</h3>
<p>If you really want the more automated way, you might be looking for a custom <strong>IL Weaver</strong> then. These basically allow to inject generated code on compile time without you having to manually write it all out.</p>
<p>You can look at <a href="https://github.com/mewlist/MewWeaver" rel="nofollow noreferrer">MewWeaver</a> (it's a bit old though) which can give you a hint where to start. Their example use case is even related to having an attribute for a method to be Profile marker wrapped ;)</p>
<p>It depends on the package <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.nuget.mono-cecil@1.11/manual/index.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>com.unity.nuget.mono-cecil</code></a></p>
<p>So basically you implement your own <code>ILPostProcessor</code> in an <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.3/Documentation/Manual/assembly-definitions-creating.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>AssemblyDefinition</code></a> that is compiled only for the Editor and at least references the two assemblies</p>
<ul>
<li><code>Unity.CompilationPipeline.Common</code></li>
<li><code>Mono.Cecil</code></li>
</ul>
<p>There you can then basically scan for the attribute and wrap methods of that type accordingly. You won't find them in the official API but they basically ship along with Unity.</p>
<p><a href="https://gio.blue/blog/28feb24-unity-ilpp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">This</a> is also a good general explanation.</p>
<p>So in summary it would be something like this</p>
<ul>
<li>install the mentioned package by name <code>com.unity.nuget.mono-cecil</code></li>
<li>have two custom assemblies
<ul>
<li>runtime
<ul>
<li>here put a custom attribute like e.g.
<pre><code>[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Class)]
public class ProfileAttribute : Attribute
{
}
</code></pre>
</li>
<li>and also the mentioned profiler Market scope like e g.
<pre><code>using System;
using Unity.Profiling;

public struct ProfileScope : IDisposable
{
#if UNITY_EDITOR
    private ProfilerMarker.AutoScope _scope;

    public ProfileScope(string name)
    {
        var marker = new ProfilerMarker(name);
        _scope = marker.Auto();
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _scope.Dispose();
    }
#else
    public ProfileScope(string name) { }
    public void Dispose() { }
#endif
}
</code></pre>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>editor
<ul>
<li>as said has to reference the two hidden assemblies</li>
<li>the IL Weaver code generation - the following I just searched together on the phone but something like e.g.
<pre><code>#if UNITY_EDITOR
using System.Linq;
using System.IO;
using Mono.Cecil;
using Mono.Cecil.Cil;
using Unity.CompilationPipeline.Common.ILPostProcessing;
using Unity.CompilationPipeline.Common.Diagnostics;

public class ProfileILPostProcessor : ILPostProcessor
{
     public override bool WillProcess(ICompiledAssembly compiledAssembly)
     {
         return compiledAssembly.Name == &quot;Assembly-CSharp&quot;;
     }

     public override ILPostProcessResult Process(ICompiledAssembly compiledAssembly)
     {
         var resolver = new DefaultAssemblyResolver();

         foreach (var path in compiledAssembly.References)
             resolver.AddSearchDirectory(Path.GetDirectoryName(path));

         var readerParams = new ReaderParameters
         {
             AssemblyResolver = resolver
         };

         var assembly = AssemblyDefinition.ReadAssembly(
             new MemoryStream(compiledAssembly.InMemoryAssembly.PeData),
             readerParams);

         var module = assembly.MainModule;

         bool modified = false;

         foreach (var type in module.Types)
         {
             if (!HasProfileAttribute(type))
                 continue;

             foreach (var method in type.Methods)
             {
                 if (ShouldSkip(method))
                     continue;

                 if (AlreadyInjected(method))
                     continue;

                 string sampleName = $&quot;{type.Name}.{method.Name}&quot;;

                if (InjectScope(method, module, sampleName))
                     modified = true;
             }
         }

         if (!modified)
             return null;

         var peStream = new MemoryStream();
         assembly.Write(peStream);

         return new ILPostProcessResult(
             new InMemoryAssembly(peStream.ToArray(), compiledAssembly.InMemoryAssembly.PdbData),
             new DiagnosticMessage[0]);
     }

     private bool HasProfileAttribute(TypeDefinition type)
     {
         return type.CustomAttributes.Any(a =&gt;
             a.AttributeType.Name == &quot;ProfileAttribute&quot;);
     }

     private bool ShouldSkip(MethodDefinition method)
     {
         return method.IsConstructor ||
             method.IsAbstract ||
             method.IsPInvokeImpl ||
             !method.HasBody;
     }

     private bool AlreadyInjected(MethodDefinition method)
     {
         return method.Body.Instructions.Any(i =&gt;
             i.OpCode == OpCodes.Newobj &amp;&amp;
             i.Operand is MethodReference mr &amp;&amp;
             mr.DeclaringType.Name == nameof(ProfileScope));
     }

     private bool InjectScope(MethodDefinition method, ModuleDefinition module, string sampleName)
     {
        var il = method.Body.GetILProcessor();
        method.Body.SimplifyMacros();

        var scopeType = module.ImportReference(typeof(ProfileScope));
        var ctor = module.ImportReference(
            typeof(ProfileScope).GetConstructor(new[] { typeof(string) }));
        var dispose = module.ImportReference(
           typeof(System.IDisposable).GetMethod(nameof(System.IDisposable.Dispose)));

        var first = method.Body.Instructions.First();

        var scopeVar = new VariableDefinition(scopeType);
        method.Body.Variables.Add(scopeVar);
        method.Body.InitLocals = true;

        // Create scope
        var loadName = il.Create(OpCodes.Ldstr, sampleName);
        var newObj = il.Create(OpCodes.Newobj, ctor);
        var store = il.Create(OpCodes.Stloc, scopeVar);

        il.InsertBefore(first, loadName);
        il.InsertAfter(loadName, newObj);
        il.InsertAfter(newObj, store);

        var tryStart = store.Next;

        // Create END label
        var endOfMethod = il.Create(OpCodes.Nop);

        // Replace all returns
        var retInstructions = method.Body.Instructions
           .Where(i =&gt; i.OpCode == OpCodes.Ret)
           .ToList();

        foreach (var ret in retInstructions)
        {
            var leave = il.Create(OpCodes.Leave, endOfMethod);
            il.Replace(ret, leave);
        }

        // FINALLY block
        var finallyStart = il.Create(OpCodes.Ldloc, scopeVar);
        il.Append(finallyStart);
        il.Append(il.Create(OpCodes.Callvirt, dispose));
        il.Append(il.Create(OpCodes.Endfinally));

        // END label
        il.Append(endOfMethod);
        il.Append(il.Create(OpCodes.Ret));

        var handler = new ExceptionHandler(ExceptionHandlerType.Finally)
        {
            TryStart = tryStart,
            TryEnd = finallyStart,
            HandlerStart = finallyStart,
            HandlerEnd = endOfMethod
        };

        method.Body.ExceptionHandlers.Add(handler);

        method.Body.OptimizeMacros();

        return true;
    }
}
#endif
</code></pre>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Something like this could then be used simply as attribute on your class scope</p>
<pre><code>[Profile]
public class Example
{
    public void ExampleA()
    {

    }

    public void ExampleB()
    {

    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>and each method would simply get wrapped by an additional</p>
<pre><code>using (new ProfileScope(&quot;ClassName.MethodName&quot;))
</code></pre>
<p>or to be exact (after all <code>using</code> is just a high level syntax sugar) rather an equivalent</p>
<pre><code>var scope = new ProfileScope(&quot;ClassName.MethodName&quot;);
try
{
    // original method body
}
finally
{
    scope.Dispose();
}
</code></pre>
